{"is_error":false,"duration_api_ms":253259,"num_turns":1,"stop_reason":"end_turn","session_id":"651ef804-1a87-4d62-9a6f-847c71c64274","total_cost_usd":1.19123,"usage":{"input_tokens":2,"cache_creation_input_tokens":58722,"cache_read_input_tokens":20164,"output_tokens":22080,"server_tool_use":{"web_search_requests":0,"web_fetch_requests":0},"service_tier":"standard","cache_creation":{"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":58722,"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":0},"inference_geo":"not_available","iterations":[{"input_tokens":2,"output_tokens":22080,"cache_read_input_tokens":20164,"cache_creation_input_tokens":58722,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":0,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":58722},"type":"message"}],"speed":"standard"},"modelUsage":{"claude-haiku-4-5-20251001":{"inputTokens":41823,"outputTokens":19,"cacheReadInputTokens":0,"cacheCreationInputTokens":0,"webSearchRequests":0,"costUSD":0.041918,"contextWindow":200000,"maxOutputTokens":32000,"canonicalModel":"claude-haiku-4-5","provider":"firstParty"},"claude-opus-5":{"inputTokens":2,"outputTokens":22080,"cacheReadInputTokens":20164,"cacheCreationInputTokens":58722,"webSearchRequests":0,"costUSD":1.1493120000000001,"contextWindow":1000000,"maxOutputTokens":64000,"canonicalModel":"claude-opus-5","provider":"firstParty"}},"permission_denials":[],"terminal_reason":"completed","fast_mode_state":"off","fast_mode_disabled_reason":"sdk_opt_in_required","subtype":"success","api_error_status":null,"result":"{\n  \"agent\": \"spec-auditor\",\n  \"verdict\": \"fail\",\n  \"summary\": \"Four of sixteen checks fail: the flown full hold is 3.01x dry mass instead of 2x, the ablation curve's 3-pass optimum is arithmetically correct but never realised in flight (cheapest is 1 pass in all nine measured descents), a full hold touches down at 5.78 m/s over the 5.0 soft line, and the doubling rule only passed because the mass anchor itself was moved from 500 kg to 45 kg — which is also what collapsed the aerobraking ladder.\",\n  \"checks\": [\n    {\n      \"rule_id\": \"band_names_defined_in_baseline\",\n      \"gdd_ref\": \"2.3.7\",\n      \"statement\": \"Every band name used in the catalog or the params appears in the baseline's bands, and no undefined band is referenced.\",\n      \"result\": \"pass\",\n      \"evidence\": \"Baseline defines exactly {suborbital, low, high}. Catalog uses those three in all 27 debris entries and in all 3 band_summary entries. Params reference them in ablation.fixed_toll_per_pass_pct_by_band, ablation.heat_index, ablation.cost_curve, ablation.optimal_pass_count and economy.band_value_multiplier — 5 maps, 3 keys each, 15 of 15 keys defined. No fourth name appears anywhere.\",\n      \"fix_hint\": \"\",\n      \"owner\": \"debris-designer\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"rule_id\": \"band_summary_matches_catalog\",\n      \"gdd_ref\": \"2.3.7\",\n      \"statement\": \"Each band_summary entry agrees with the debris array it summarises on piece_count, mean_mass_kg and fragile_fraction.\",\n      \"result\": \"pass\",\n      \"evidence\": \"Suborbital: 9 pieces, mass sum 34+12+28+6.5+9+4+8+5.5+180 = 287, mean 31.889 vs stated 31.89; fragile spawn weight (4+3)=7 of total 70 = 0.100 vs stated 0.1. Low: 9 pieces, sum 62+48+22+18+26+11+55+19+330 = 591, mean 65.667 vs stated 65.67; fragile (5+3+2)=10 of 60 = 0.1667 vs stated 0.167. High: 9 pieces, sum 96+84+44+78+21+52+58+68+480 = 981, mean 109.0 vs stated 109; fragile (6+5+4+1)=16 of 64 = 0.250 vs stated 0.25. All nine figures reconcile; fragile_fraction is spawn-weighted, not counted (by count it would be 0.222/0.333/0.444).\",\n      \"fix_hint\": \"\",\n      \"owner\": \"debris-designer\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"rule_id\": \"mean_mass_rises_by_band\",\n      \"gdd_ref\": \"2.3.7\",\n      \"statement\": \"The catalog's mean piece mass rises band over band, so valuable junk physically fights the player.\",\n      \"result\": \"pass\",\n      \"evidence\": \"31.89 -> 65.67 -> 109.0 kg, ratios 2.06x and 1.66x, 3.42x bottom to top. Excluding the one oversized outlier per band, the hand-tetherable population runs 877/8 = 109.6... per-piece spawn-weighted 12.71 / 36.56 / 61.9 kg, still monotonic and a 4.9x climb. Rising in both readings.\",\n      \"fix_hint\": \"\",\n      \"owner\": \"debris-designer\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"rule_id\": \"fragile_share_rises_by_band\",\n      \"gdd_ref\": \"2.3.7\",\n      \"statement\": \"Fragile spawn share rises across the bands, roughly 1 in 10 suborbital to 1 in 4 high.\",\n      \"result\": \"pass\",\n      \"evidence\": \"Spawn-weighted fragile share 7/70 = 0.100 (1 in 10.0), 10/60 = 0.167 (1 in 6.0), 16/64 = 0.250 (1 in 4.0). Monotonic, and the two endpoints land exactly on the GDD's 1-in-10 and 1-in-4.\",\n      \"fix_hint\": \"\",\n      \"owner\": \"debris-designer\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"rule_id\": \"full_hold_doubles_mass\",\n      \"gdd_ref\": \"2.3.5\",\n      \"statement\": \"A full hold roughly doubles the rocket's mass at starting storage size.\",\n      \"result\": \"fail\",\n      \"evidence\": \"Recomputed over the hand-tetherable shipping-slice population at compactor tier 1 (smalls crush to 1 slot, fragile smalls 2, mediums 4): suborbital weighted mass 877 over weighted slots 118 = 7.43 kg/slot; low band 2157 over 167 = 12.92 kg/slot; the two bands combined 3034/285 = 10.65 kg/slot. Six base slots therefore hold 44.6 kg (suborbital-only, 1.99x dry_mass_kg 45), 77.5 kg (low-only, 2.72x) or 63.9 kg (slice mix, 2.42x). The simulator flew 15.09 kg/slot, a 90.6 kg hold and a 3.01x mass ratio — 50% past doubling and outside the 25% tolerance. The params' 1.99x holds only if every piece the player ever carries is suborbital; the flown mix is a band up.\",\n      \"fix_hint\": \"Raise dry_mass_kg toward ~90 so that the flown 90.6 kg hold lands near 2x, rather than leaving it anchored to the suborbital-only mix.\",\n      \"owner\": \"economy-balancer\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"rule_id\": \"ablation_optimum_two_to_four_passes\",\n      \"gdd_ref\": \"2.3.1\",\n      \"statement\": \"The ablation cost curve puts the cheapest descent at 2 to 4 committed passes from every band, never one plunge and never feathering.\",\n      \"result\": \"fail\",\n      \"evidence\": \"The array and the model agree. Recomputed from n*toll + 0.00148*n*(H/n)^2.2: suborbital n=1 gives 4 + 0.00148*25118.9 = 41.18 (array 41.18), n=2 gives 8 + 0.00296*5466.8 = 24.18 (array 24.18), n=3 gives 12 + 0.00444*2240.5 = 21.95 (array 21.95), n=8 gives 32 + 0.01184*258.9 = 35.07 (array 35.07). Low n=1 = 4.6 + 45.30 = 49.90 (array 49.9), n=3 = 13.8 + 12.12 = 25.92 (array 25.92). High n=1 = 5.8 + 63.11 = 68.91 (array 68.92), n=3 = 17.4 + 16.89 = 34.29 (array 34.29), n=4 = 23.2 + 11.96 = 35.16 (array 35.16). All three minima sit at index 3 and match optimal_pass_count 3/3/3. The check fails on the rule, not the arithmetic: flown, cheapest_pass_count is 1 in all nine band x load descents, and measured ablation rises monotonically with pass count wherever a second count is reachable (low empty 44.1/64.6/85.7 for 1/2/3; high full hold 188.2/232.3/252.3/262.0/264.8 for 1-5). Single-pass measurement reproduces the authored curve to within 0.05 points (41.2 vs 41.18), so the formula shipped as written — what does not exist is the cheap multi-pass trajectory the formula assumes. The controlling quantity is the staged ballistic coefficient, measured at 8.9 kg/m2 against the baseline's 98.5, which puts every multi-pass brake at 77-88 m inside a 90 m atmosphere.\",\n      \"fix_hint\": \"The lever is the mass-to-reference-area scale that sets ballistic coefficient (dry_mass_kg against the baseline's reference_area_m2 3.5), not heat_cost_coefficient or the tolls — no ablation coefficient can create a braking depth the trajectory does not offer.\",\n      \"owner\": \"economy-balancer\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"rule_id\": \"parachute_descent_under_soft_line\",\n      \"gdd_ref\": \"2.3.2\",\n      \"statement\": \"Parachute descent speed at full hold is under the 5 m/s soft-landing threshold and above about 3.5 m/s, so the Parachute upgrade is a real purchase.\",\n      \"result\": \"fail\",\n      \"evidence\": \"descent_speed_full_hold_ms is declared 4.6, inside the 3.5-5.0 window. Flown, touchdown is 3.28 m/s empty, 4.68 at half hold and 5.78 at full hold, with soft_landing false for all three full-hold descents. The sqrt-of-mass law itself is exact — 3.28 * sqrt(135.6/45) = 5.69 against the measured 5.78 — so the relation holds and only the anchor mass is wrong: the 4.6 figure was solved at 90 kg all-up (twice dry mass) while the flown full hold is 45 + 90.6 = 135.6 kg. Restoring 4.6 at the real full hold needs Cd*A up by (5.78/4.6)^2 = 1.579, i.e. parachute_drag_coefficient from 1.75 to about 2.76 — past the current tier-1 upgrade value of 2.55.\",\n      \"fix_hint\": \"Raise landing.parachute_drag_coefficient to about 2.8 at base (or state landing.parachute_area_m2 explicitly and size it to the real 135.6 kg laden mass), and re-space the two parachute tiers above it.\",\n      \"owner\": \"economy-balancer\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"rule_id\": \"tow_fee_clamps_at_half\",\n      \"gdd_ref\": \"2.3.3\",\n      \"statement\": \"The tow fee is zero inside the free radius, linear beyond it, clamps at exactly 50%, and can never go negative or exceed the cap.\",\n      \"result\": \"pass\",\n      \"evidence\": \"free_radius_m 160, max_fee_fraction 0.5, curve linear. Half circumference = pi*800 = 2513.27 m. fee(d) = 0.5*(d-160)/(2513.27-160) clamped to [0, 0.5]: d=0 -> -0.034 clamped to 0; d=160 -> exactly 0; d=1336.6 (midpoint) -> 0.2500; d=2513.27 -> exactly 0.5000; d beyond the antipode -> clamped at 0.5. Payout therefore runs 100% at the pad to 50% at the far side, never below. Applied to haul value only, so it cannot reach savings.\",\n      \"fix_hint\": \"\",\n      \"owner\": \"economy-balancer\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"rule_id\": \"lazy_run_breaks_even\",\n      \"gdd_ref\": \"2.3.6\",\n      \"statement\": \"Launch cost is at or below the value of three cheap suborbital pieces, so a lazy run still breaks even.\",\n      \"result\": \"pass\",\n      \"evidence\": \"Cheapest suborbital piece = size_class_base_value.small 22 x band_value_multiplier.suborbital 1.0 = 22. Three of them = 66. launch_cost 48 <= 66, and 48/22 = 2.18 pieces, inside the GDD's 'about the value of 2-3 pieces of cheap junk'. Caveat carried into observations rather than failed here: the second half of the launch bill is replating, and the measured cheapest suborbital descent costs 41.2% of plate empty and 93.0% at half hold, so at replate_cost_per_pct 0.6 a half-hold lazy run pays 48 + 55.8 = 103.8 against roughly 63 of cargo. The launch-cost arithmetic this check specifies passes; the ablation measurement is what breaks the economics, and it is failed under ablation_optimum_two_to_four_passes.\",\n      \"fix_hint\": \"\",\n      \"owner\": \"economy-balancer\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"rule_id\": \"steady_tow_never_shears\",\n      \"gdd_ref\": \"2.3.4\",\n      \"statement\": \"Steady-state towing tension never reaches the magnet hold force, so shear fires only on jerk.\",\n      \"result\": \"pass\",\n      \"evidence\": \"a = jetpack_thrust_n / (suit_mass_kg + towed mass) = 200/(20+M); tension_i = m_i * a. Heaviest legal two-piece load in the whole catalog (2 base tether slots): rtg_core_shell 96 kg + tungsten_ballast_slug 84 kg, M = 180, a = 200/200 = 1.000 m/s2, so tensions are 96.0 N and 84.0 N against magnet_hold_force_n 260 — 2.7x margin. Worst single tether is the same 96 kg piece alone: 96*200/116 = 165.5 N, 1.57x margin. Heaviest slice-legal pair (satellite_bus_frame 62 + thruster_quad_cluster 55): a = 200/137 = 1.460, tensions 90.5 N and 80.3 N. The hard asymptote is thrust itself — tension_i < 200 N for any load at any tether count — which is 0.77 of the 260 N base hold force, so steady towing cannot shear anywhere in the catalog.\",\n      \"fix_hint\": \"\",\n      \"owner\": \"economy-balancer\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"rule_id\": \"staging_is_one_way\",\n      \"gdd_ref\": \"2.3.1\",\n      \"statement\": \"No parameter reintroduces main or RCS thrust after staging.\",\n      \"result\": \"pass\",\n      \"evidence\": \"The only thrust fields in the params are flight.thrust_n 4200, flight.rcs_thrust_n 210 (both properties of the stage that is jettisoned, both metered by flight.fuel_capacity_kg 33 which goes with the tank) and eva.jetpack_thrust_n 200, which acts on the astronaut. The reentry, ablation and landing blocks contain no force or delta-v field. None of the 12 upgrades touches post-stage propulsion — fuel_tank, thruster, storage, heat_shield, parachute, hand_magnet. The jetpack against a 135.6 kg laden ship gives 1.47 m/s2, a rescue nudge as 2.3.1 specifies, not an engine.\",\n      \"fix_hint\": \"\",\n      \"owner\": \"economy-balancer\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"rule_id\": \"fragile_never_crushable_and_premium_real\",\n      \"gdd_ref\": \"2.3.4\",\n      \"statement\": \"Fragile pieces are never crushable at any compactor tier present, and the fragile premium makes one intact fragile piece worth several solid pieces of the same class and band.\",\n      \"result\": \"pass\",\n      \"evidence\": \"cargo.compactor_tier is 1 (smalls only) and no params field grants a fragile exemption or a crush override; the catalog carries slot costs per class only, with the fragile flag orthogonal, so nothing in the value set can crush glass. Premium: fragile_value_premium 4.0 means a star_tracker_head (low, small, fragile) prices at 22 * 2.6 * 4 = 228.8 against 57.2 for a solid low small — 4 pieces' worth, the GDD's 'several'. Per slot it is 114.4 vs 57.2 because it pays 2 uncrushed slots forever. cracked_solar_array prices at 80 * 2.6 * 4 = 832 over 4 slots = 208/slot against 52/slot for a solid low medium.\",\n      \"fix_hint\": \"\",\n      \"owner\": \"economy-balancer\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"rule_id\": \"oversized_rejects_hand_magnet\",\n      \"gdd_ref\": \"2.3.4\",\n      \"statement\": \"Oversized junk rejects the hand magnet and can only be taken with the crane cable.\",\n      \"result\": \"pass\",\n      \"evidence\": \"size_classes.oversized.hand_tetherable = false; small and medium are true. The three oversized entries — derelict_upper_stage 180 kg, collapsed_boom_mast 330 kg, armstrongs_satellite 480 kg, one per band at spawn_weight 1 — are therefore unreachable without the crane, which the slice does not ship. Slot costs 6 crushed / 12 uncrushed exceed the 6-slot base hold, consistent with 'most of a hold'.\",\n      \"fix_hint\": \"\",\n      \"owner\": \"debris-designer\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"rule_id\": \"twelve_upgrade_purchases\",\n      \"gdd_ref\": \"2.3.6\",\n      \"statement\": \"Twelve upgrade purchases: six parts, two tiers each, tier 2 dearer than tier 1, and each tier improving the stat it names.\",\n      \"result\": \"pass\",\n      \"evidence\": \"12 entries across 6 parts x 2 tiers. Costs rise within every part: fuel_tank 180->520, thruster 200->560, storage 240->700, heat_shield 260->720, parachute 220->620, hand_magnet 190->540; tree total 4950. Each named stat improves from its base: fuel_capacity_kg 33->48->62, thrust_n 4200->5400->6800, base_slots 6->9->12, plate_capacity_pct 100->140->190, parachute_drag_coefficient 1.75->2.55->4.2 (higher Cd = slower descent), and hand_magnet splits its two tiers across the two stats 4.1 says ship — magnet_range_screens 0.125->0.1875 at tier 1 and magnet_hold_force_n 260->380 at tier 2.\",\n      \"fix_hint\": \"\",\n      \"owner\": \"economy-balancer\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"rule_id\": \"shipping_slice_bands_populated\",\n      \"gdd_ref\": \"4.1\",\n      \"statement\": \"The suborbital and low bands each carry at least one fragile piece and at least two size classes, so the player meets crushing and fragility inside the slice.\",\n      \"result\": \"pass\",\n      \"evidence\": \"Suborbital: 6 small, 2 medium, 1 oversized (3 classes) with 2 fragile pieces (cracked_antenna_dish, camera_optics_pod), and 4 crushable non-fragile smalls so tier-1 crushing is met immediately. Low: 3 small, 5 medium, 1 oversized (3 classes) with 3 fragile pieces (cracked_solar_array, star_tracker_head, dish_reflector_petal). Both bands are also flyable as measured: suborbital reached with 3.2 kg / 9.7% fuel margin, low reached with 1.06 kg / 3.2%; high not reached, which is the deferred band.\",\n      \"fix_hint\": \"\",\n      \"owner\": \"debris-designer\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"rule_id\": \"no_target_moved\",\n      \"gdd_ref\": \"2.2\",\n      \"statement\": \"No rule passes only because a quantity the design document fixes, or a quantity the rule is measured against, was moved.\",\n      \"result\": \"fail\",\n      \"evidence\": \"The GDD's prose-fixed numbers all held: base_slots 6 (2.2's 'Cargo reads 4 of 6' and the appendix's 2-tethers-vs-6-slots question), base_tether_slots 2, soft_landing_ms 5.0, magnet_range_screens 0.125, compactor_tier 1, no_gear_multiplier 2, fragile_multiplier 2, cargo_damage_interval_s 3, heat_dissipation_s 5, max_fee_fraction 0.5, 12 purchases. Two measured-against quantities did move. (a) dry_mass_kg is 45 against the baseline's stated ship dry mass of 500 kg, an 11.1x reduction, and it is the sole reason the doubling arithmetic lands at 1.99x. Every drag and heat number in the baseline scales as Cd*A/m, so at the baseline's reference_area_m2 3.5 the staged ballistic coefficient falls from 98.5 to a measured 8.9 kg/m2 — the same 11.1x — which is what put every multi-pass brake at 77-88 m inside a 90 m atmosphere and killed the ladder that ablation_optimum_two_to_four_passes depends on. The mass rule was made true by moving the mass. (b) descent_speed_full_hold_ms 4.6 is named for a full hold but was solved at 90 kg all-up, i.e. 45 kg of cargo, which is half of the flown 90.6 kg hold; the simulator had to infer parachute_area_m2 = 48.6 from the claim because the params never state the canopy. The soft-landing rule passed on paper because 'full hold' was redefined as half a hold.\",\n      \"fix_hint\": \"Fix the mass scale first — either restore dry_mass_kg near the baseline's 500 kg or publish a reference_area_m2 consistent with 45 kg (about 0.315 m2) — and re-derive descent_speed_full_hold_ms against the actual 6-slot hold rather than against twice dry mass.\",\n      \"owner\": \"economy-balancer\"\n    }\n  ],\n  \"observations\": [\n    \"reentry.heat_capacity is 100 and no upgrade in the set moves it — both heat_shield tiers change plate_capacity_pct only. Measured peak heat is 149/153/164 at half hold and 187/192/206 at full hold, so every laden return from every band pins the bar and takes guaranteed cargo damage. This is the one reentry cost the player cannot buy margin against, which reads as an unfair tax rather than a consequence of flying badly.\",\n    \"The cheapest full-hold descent burns 151.1% (suborbital), 161.3% (low) and 188.2% (high) of a 100% plate, and a high-band half hold already costs 115.8%. At base shield a full hold cannot be returned from the starting band; at maximum shield (190) the high-band full hold clears by 1.8 points with no margin for an attitude error. Passing the audit's ablation-curve arithmetic did not make the shield survivable.\",\n    \"A shallow unstaged braking pass peaks at 696 (suborbital) and 734 (low) against a 100 bar — roughly 7x, not the derived 3.2x. The pre-stage coarse-braking phase 2.3.1 calls 'the skill is when' does not exist in this configuration; staging becomes a mandatory prerequisite rather than a commit, which removes the timing decision the section is built around.\",\n    \"soft_landing_ms 5.0 was correctly kept as a GDD key value, but at g = 30 a 0.42 m free fall already reaches it. The chute must hold the ship all the way to contact and any bounce is an automatic hard landing. Widening to about 8 m/s is a one-field change if it feels unfair in the hands; that is a design call, not a tuning one.\",\n    \"tungsten_ballast_slug is a 1-slot small at 84 kg. It ships post-semester, but a Storage tier 2 hold of twelve of them is 1008 kg on a 45 kg ship and lands at roughly 15.8 m/s even at Parachute tier 2 — no legal chute value covers it. Either its mass or its size class wants revisiting before the high band is built.\",\n    \"armstrongs_satellite at 480 kg is 10.7x the 45 kg ship, against 2.3.7's 'roughly doubles the ship's mass on its own'. At the mass scale the rest of the catalog implies, the two numbers cannot both stand.\",\n    \"At compactor tier 1 a medium costs 4 uncrushed slots of a 6-slot hold, so two mediums cannot fit a starting hold at all. The player's first real towing lesson — bent_truss_section and spent_booster_ring, whose notes say teaching towing is their purpose — is also the haul that most wastes the hold.\",\n    \"The low band arrives with 1.06 kg of fuel and a 3.2% margin on the base ship. 2.3.7 wants the band gated by the Fuel Tank purchase; a 3.2% margin gates it on pilot precision instead, so the band opens for a good pilot before it opens for a paying one.\",\n    \"The params never state landing.parachute_area_m2 or a reference area consistent with dry_mass_kg. Both were inferred downstream — the canopy from descent_speed_full_hold_ms, the drag area from the baseline's 3.5 m2. Any quantity the value set does not state is a quantity the next agent guesses.\"\n  ]\n}","ttft_ms":160987,"ttft_stream_ms":2639,"time_to_request_ms":417,"type":"result","duration_ms":251753,"uuid":"4e79b929-7087-4e7d-9956-8ab2860b8fd7"}
